(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with deeply pigmented skin has months of itching and swelling of both lower legs. The gaiter regions show poorly demarcated violaceous-to-deep-brown patches, and on palpation both areas are warm and boggy. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stasis dermatitis","Bilateral cellulitis of the lower legs","Allergic contact dermatitis to a topical product","Nummular eczema of both lower extremities"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — on darker skin the erythema reads as violaceous, grey or brown, so palpation for warmth and oedema carries the diagnosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"A 29-year-old hairdresser has a three-week history of intensely itchy small blisters along the sides of her fingers and on her palms. Examination shows grouped 1 to 2 mm vesicles described as resembling tapioca, on a poorly defined erythematous base, with surrounding dryness. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dyshidrotic eczema","Allergic contact dermatitis","Bullous pemphigoid","Nummular eczema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tapioca vesicles on the palms and lateral fingers are the defining picture; vesicles may coalesce into bullae before desquamating. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 3-month-old infant has thick, greasy yellow scale adherent to the scalp, with mild erythema and no distress. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis","Tinea capitis","Psoriasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the scalp form is dandruff in adults and this presentation in infants, driven by Malassezia in sebum-rich areas. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has a two-month history of intense itching followed by blisters on the trunk, axillae and inner thighs. Examination shows numerous tense bullae 1 to 3 cm across on urticarial bases; rubbing adjacent normal skin does not cause it to shear away. There is no oral involvement. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic contact dermatitis","Bullous pemphigoid","Pemphigus vulgaris","Dyshidrotic eczema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tense bullae, a negative Nikolsky sign, an itchy prodrome and onset after 60 are the picture. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 41-year-old intensive care nurse reports several weeks of worsening dryness, redness and scaling of both hands, worst over the dorsal surfaces. She washes her hands more than thirty times a shift. The rash is well demarcated with a glazed appearance and there are no vesicles. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic contact dermatitis","Dyshidrotic eczema","Psoriasis","Irritant contact dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the commonest form of contact dermatitis, frequently occupational, with soaps and detergents as classic irritants and a glazed well-demarcated appearance. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a six-week history of painful mouth erosions and now has flaccid blisters on the scalp and chest that rupture easily and bleed. Lateral pressure on adjacent normal skin causes the top layer to slough. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lichen planus","Pemphigus vulgaris","Bullous pemphigoid","Erythema multiforme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Oral onset, flaccid easily ruptured bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign are the triad, reflecting intraepithelial acantholysis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 130"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has widespread tense bullae and a clinical suspicion of bullous pemphigoid. Which test confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial culture of blister fluid","Biopsy of perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence","Potassium hydroxide preparation of the blister roof","Tzanck smear of a fresh blister"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — lesional tissue goes for histopathology and perilesional tissue for immunofluorescence, with serum studies or ELISA identifying anti-basement membrane zone antibodies. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has limited plaque psoriasis over both elbows. Which first-line topical option acts quickest, and what caution applies?","choices":["Salicylic acid — caution with systemic absorption in children","Methotrexate — caution with hepatotoxicity","Calcipotriene, a vitamin D analogue — caution with nephrotoxicity","Coal tar — caution with photosensitivity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside emollients, topical steroids and ultraviolet B phototherapy as first-line options. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 163"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has patchy hair loss with an unusual scarred appearance in one area and diffuse atypical loss elsewhere, and the picture remains unclear after dermoscopy. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin topical minoxidil","Repeat dermoscopy in six months","Scalp biopsy","Begin intralesional corticosteroids empirically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — biopsy is performed when scarring alopecia, diffuse atypical loss or persistent diagnostic uncertainty remains after clinical and dermoscopic assessment. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 139"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has suspected lichen planus. Which biopsy finding confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Neutrophils aligned in a row at the dermal-epidermal junction","Munro microabscesses within the stratum corneum","Band-like infiltration of lymphocytes in the dermis","Acantholysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis, basal vacuolisation, Civatte bodies and a saw-tooth rete ridge. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 177"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with pityriasis rosea asks what to expect. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It is self-limited and typically fades over 4 to 6 weeks, though darker skin may show post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation for several months","It requires a six-week course of oral antibiotics","It will persist indefinitely without phototherapy","It is highly contagious by direct skin contact, so he should avoid close contact with others until every last lesion has faded"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reassurance is the mainstay, with antihistamines or topical steroids used cautiously for itch. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"An 8-month-old has erythema, scaling and small erosions over the convex surfaces of the buttocks and upper thighs, with the inguinal folds clearly spared. There is no purulence. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["A topical antifungal at every change","Oral antibiotics","A high-potency topical corticosteroid applied at every change until the rash has completely cleared","Frequent diaper changes, gentle cleansing, air exposure and a thick zinc oxide or petrolatum barrier at each change"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — reducing moisture and irritant exposure is the foundation, and the fold sparing indicates irritant rather than candidal disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a single intensely itchy, well-demarcated thickened plaque on her lateral ankle with exaggerated skin markings and pigment change. She admits to scratching it most evenings while watching television. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Break the itch-scratch cycle through education, emollients, behavioural substitution and nail care, with a limited course of a potent topical steroid","Excise the plaque","Begin oral antibiotics","Begin an oral antifungal such as terbinafine, on the basis that the thickened scaly plaque is most likely a fungal infection of long standing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — recurrence is common unless the initiating itch is controlled. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has several round scaly plaques on his forearms. The lesions are uniform throughout without central clearing, and he has been applying an over-the-counter antifungal for three weeks without improvement. Which is the best initial diagnostic test?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation","Punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence","Serum immunoglobulin E level","Bacterial culture and sensitivity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tinea corporis is the main differential, and it must be excluded before committing to a diagnosis of nummular eczema. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A mother of a child newly diagnosed with atopic dermatitis is anxious about using topical steroids at all. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Advise that the condition resolves permanently by school age, so consistent daily treatment is optional and can be stopped whenever the skin looks clear","Address the concern directly, review the side effects, demonstrate how much to apply, and provide a written flare and infection action plan","Advise avoiding all topical steroids and relying on emollients alone","Advise applying the steroid generously over the whole body twice daily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Addressing steroid concerns directly and demonstrating quantity and application are named education points. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has diffuse thinning over the central and parietal scalp with her frontal hairline preserved. Which is the most appropriate treatment consideration?","choices":["Intralesional corticosteroids","Antifungal shampoo","Oral antiandrogens such as combined oral contraceptives may be considered, since treatment options are limited","Oral finasteride prescribed as first-line therapy for female-pattern hair loss at any age after puberty"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — female-pattern hair loss has fewer options, and finasteride is not indicated in women. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman with rapidly progressive alopecia areata is distressed and withdrawing socially. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The hair loss is permanent, because the follicle and its stem cells have been destroyed","The condition is contagious to household members","Nail changes exclude the diagnosis","Psychological support is one of the most important considerations, and support groups may be offered"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is named among the most important factors in managing this condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"A 44-year-old janitor has chronic dry, fissured, glazed dermatitis of both hands from repeated detergent exposure. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Begin oral doxycycline","Refer for patch testing before any treatment","Avoid the exposure and repair the barrier with emollients, sleeping in cotton gloves after applying a heavy emollient such as petroleum jelly","Begin a high-potency topical corticosteroid indefinitely, continuing it daily even once the hands have healed, without addressing the exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with antihistamines such as hydroxyzine or diphenhydramine for itch. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a soft subcutaneous nodule on the forearm and the clinician wants to know whether it is fluid-filled. Which bedside technique addresses that?","choices":["Mineral oil preparation","Transillumination","Dermoscopy","Diascopy with a Wood's lamp"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a fluid-filled lesion glows when a light is placed against it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with long-standing venous insufficiency has itchy, hyperpigmented plaques over both gaiter regions with induration. His ankle-brachial index is normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Strict bed rest with the legs dependent","Compression therapy, with leg elevation, walking and calf-muscle exercises","Long-term systemic antibiotics","High-potency topical corticosteroids as the sole long-term therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — compression is the cornerstone once arterial circulation is adequate. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has painful oral erosions and flaccid skin bullae. A biopsy has been taken. Which finding on that biopsy would establish the diagnosis?","choices":["Acantholysis","Neutrophils aligned in a straight narrow row at the dermal-epidermal junction","Band-like lymphocytic infiltration of the dermis","Hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis and Civatte bodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion is the defining histological feature, with immunofluorescence and serum ELISA confirmatory. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 132"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with known bilateral stasis dermatitis develops acute swelling and pain in the right calf over two days. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure her that unilateral flares are expected in stasis dermatitis","Order venous duplex ultrasonography to evaluate for deep venous thrombosis","Increase the strength of her compression stockings","Begin a topical corticosteroid to the affected calf"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute unilateral swelling or pain should raise concern for deep venous thrombosis in this setting. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman has adult-onset eczema that has not responded to appropriate topical therapy over several months. Her rash involves the eyelids and hands. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide preparation","Skin-prick testing","Tzanck smear","Patch testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — patch testing is indicated for atypical, adult-onset or treatment-resistant disease, and eyelid and hand involvement raises contact allergy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has plaques on both elbows that have been present for two years. The clinical picture is not clear-cut and treatment has failed. Which test would settle the diagnosis?","choices":["Serum HLA-B27","Direct immunofluorescence","Skin biopsy","Potassium hydroxide preparation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — psoriasis is generally a clinical diagnosis, though biopsy may be needed for definitive diagnosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 162"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy is brought in with a recurrent, intensely pruritic rash in the antecubital and popliteal fossae. Examination shows poorly demarcated erythematous plaques with excoriations and lichenification (thickening with exaggerated skin lines). His mother has asthma. There is no fever, drainage, crusting or tenderness. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Systemic prednisone for four weeks with a slow taper","Topical hydrocortisone with regular emollient use","Oral cephalexin","Oral terbinafine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low-potency steroid plus barrier repair is first line, and the flexural distribution with a family atopy history is classic childhood atopic dermatitis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has erosive oral and vulvar lichen planus. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Mucosal involvement resolves spontaneously and needs no follow-up","The condition is contagious to sexual partners","Hepatitis C is the established cause of this condition, so treating the infection will reliably clear the rash","Genital and erosive oral disease carry an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma, so ongoing surveillance is needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — this is the reason mucosal involvement must be identified and followed. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has extensive perioral dermatitis that has persisted despite stopping her topical corticosteroid and simplifying her routine. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical metronidazole","Oral prednisone","Topical high-potency corticosteroid","Oral tetracycline or doxycycline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — more extensive or persistent disease may require an oral tetracycline. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has thick psoriatic plaques on both elbows that have not responded to a moderate potency steroid. Which agent from the potency table is the highest available?","choices":["Clobetasol propionate 0.05%","Betamethasone valerate 0.1%","Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05%","Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is the only agent in the high potency tier on this table. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 26-year-old developed an itchy rash confined to a rectangle on her forearm, sparing a small central area, three days after a minor injury. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The rash is contagious and she should avoid close contact until it clears","The reaction indicates the wound is infected and needs antibiotics","Future exposure to the same adhesive will not provoke a reaction once this episode has settled","The reaction is to the adhesive in the dressing, and the spared centre corresponds to the cotton pad"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the shape reproducing the object is the hallmark of allergic contact dermatitis to adhesives. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 46-year-old with known plaque psoriasis stops a course of oral prednisone prescribed elsewhere. Days later she develops fever for several days, then an abrupt generalised eruption of 2 to 3 mm pustules over the trunk and limbs including the palms and nail beds, some coalescing into lakes of pus. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Restart the oral prednisone at the previous dose and then continue it indefinitely thereafter","Reassure and review in two weeks, since psoriasis flares are expected","Recognise this as pustular psoriasis of the Von Zumbusch type and treat it as a life-threatening emergency","Begin oral antibiotics for a presumed bacterial pustular infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is abrupt and life-threatening, may follow systemic steroid withdrawal, and is commonly mistaken for bacterial or viral infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"}]};
